reagent-separation-group-ks-petroleum = petroleum

reagent-name-ks-refinery-gas = refinery gas
reagent-desc-ks-refinery-gas = The lightest end of crude oil, mostly propane and butane. Liquid only under pressure or in the cold.
reagent-name-ks-ethane = ethane
reagent-desc-ks-ethane = A light hydrocarbon gas. Boils so close to ethylene that splitting the two takes a tall, cold column.
reagent-name-ks-ethylene = ethylene
reagent-desc-ks-ethylene = The feedstock of the plastics industry. Made by cracking heavier hydrocarbons in steam.

reagent-name-ks-naphtha = naphtha
reagent-desc-ks-naphtha = A light, volatile cut of crude oil. Feed for reforming into gasoline, or cracking into ethylene.
reagent-name-ks-gasoline = gasoline
reagent-desc-ks-gasoline = High-octane motor fuel. Burns better than anything else a generator will take.
reagent-name-ks-kerosene = kerosene
reagent-desc-ks-kerosene = A middle cut of crude oil, and a decent fuel.
reagent-name-ks-diesel = diesel
reagent-desc-ks-diesel = A heavy fuel cut. Turns cloudy and sluggish in the cold.

reagent-name-ks-gas-oil = vacuum gas oil
reagent-desc-ks-gas-oil = Heavy oil that cracks into coke before it boils at one atmosphere, so it only distils under vacuum.
reagent-name-ks-paraffin-wax = paraffin wax
reagent-desc-ks-paraffin-wax = Long straight-chain hydrocarbons. Stays dissolved in warm oil and crystallises out when chilled.
reagent-name-ks-bitumen = bitumen
reagent-desc-ks-bitumen = The black residue left at the very bottom of the barrel. Solid unless hot, and glad to clog a pipe.
reagent-name-ks-organosulfur = organosulfur compounds
reagent-desc-ks-organosulfur = The sulfur in crude oil. Boils with kerosene, so it won't distil out, and it ruins platinum catalysts.
reagent-name-ks-polyethylene = polyethylene
reagent-desc-ks-polyethylene = Molten plastic. Sets hard wherever it lands.

reagent-name-ks-hydrogen-sulfide = hydrogen sulfide
reagent-desc-ks-hydrogen-sulfide = A toxic gas that smells of rotten eggs, until it deadens your sense of smell.
reagent-name-ks-petroleum-coke = petroleum coke
reagent-desc-ks-petroleum-coke = Hard black carbon left by oil cooked too hot. Stays put in a pipe until it is burned out with oxygen.
